  "body": {
    "id": "prop:two-environment-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "In the special case \\(d=1\\) and \\(E=2\\), \\(\\operatorname{rank}(C)=1\\) implies \\(A=\\mathbb R\\) and \\(Q_A=0\\), so the finite-breakout branch \\(\\mathcal B\\) is empty.  For every \\(\\theta\\in\\mathcal N_\\rho\\), the legal pair \\(\\theta,S\\theta\\) has the same observed laws \\(\\{P_e:e\\in\\mathcal E\\}\\) and the distinct effects \\(\\beta\\) and \\(\\beta+\\gamma/\\alpha\\), reproducing the simultaneous-noise nonidentification of Kivva et al., Theorem 3.6, whenever this branch is nonempty.",
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:calibration-rank",
      "def:source-swap",
      "thm:identification-frontier",
      "thm:environment-sharpness"
    ],
    "justification": "This reduction checks that the new frontier collapses to the closest known result at the two-environment boundary.",
    "gap": "This is already known from Kivva et al., Theorem 3.6, and is included only as a fidelity reduction rather than a novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "It lets users interpret the new condition as a strict joint-environment rescue of the established two-environment obstruction.",
    "proof_tex": "Assume \\(d=1\\) and \\(E=2\\).  Then \\(E=d+1\\).  By the rank assumption \\(\\operatorname{rank}(C)=d=1\\), the hypotheses of \\(\\text{thm:environment-sharpness}\\) apply.  Its boundary conclusion gives\n\\[\nA=\\mathbb R^d=\\mathbb R\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nQ_A=0.\n\\]\nEquivalently, writing the one-by-one calibration matrix as \\(C=[c_2]\\), rank one implies \\(c_2\\ne0\\), so \\(C^\\top C=c_2^2>0\\) and\n\\[\nQ_A\n=I_1-C(C^\\top C)^{-1}C^\\top\n=1-c_2(c_2^2)^{-1}c_2\n=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(Q_Ac_k=0\\) for every integer \\(k\\ge3=d+2\\).  Hence the set \\(\\{k\\ge d+2:Q_Ac_k\\ne0\\}\\) is empty and the first-breakout convention yields \\(r^\\star=\\infty\\).  In particular no parameter satisfying the calibration condition can have a finite breakout, and therefore the finite-breakout branch obeys\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\varnothing.\n\\]\n\nNow let \\(\\theta\\in\\mathcal N_\\rho\\).  The already established \\(\\text{thm:identification-frontier}\\), applied to this no-breakout parameter, states that \\(\\theta\\) and \\(S\\theta\\) induce the same observed law in every environment:\n\\[\nP_e^{\\theta}=P_e^{S\\theta}\n\\qquad(e\\in\\mathcal E).\n\\]\nBy the source-swap definition, their direct treatment effects are respectively\n\\[\n\\beta(\\theta)=\\beta,\n\\qquad\n\\beta(S\\theta)=\\beta+\\frac{\\gamma}{\\alpha}.\n\\]\nThe same frontier theorem states that these two effects are distinct; its conclusion uses the maintained nonzero-confounding bound in the ambient model.  Moreover, membership in \\(\\mathcal N_\\rho\\) makes this a legal swap pair, and the theorem places both representations in \\(\\mathcal D_\\rho\\).  Consequently every nonempty surviving no-breakout branch exhibits observational equivalence with unequal causal effects.  This is exactly the simultaneous-source ambiguity asserted by the proposition at the two-environment boundary; the reference to Kivva et al., Theorem 3.6, is a comparison of scope and is not used as a proof premise.",
